  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy today, otherwise seasonable through Wednesday.\n\nA sfc to mid-level trough will cross the area later today with\nthe only sensible impacts being enhancement of NW winds, which\nare expected to gust to around 35 mph. The trough lacks any\nthermal or moisture gradients, so temperatures are expected to\nbe similar to yesterday. \n\nA warm front will lift through the area late tonight or early\nTuesday with temperatures expected to rise 5 to 10 degs from\ntoday, which should put some areas into the low 70s.\n\nGenerally milder conditions with less wind are expected Tuesday\nand Wednesday. High temperatures each afternoon are expected to\nreach well into the 60s to perhaps lower 70s (with 50s at higher\nelevations). Very light winds and mainly clear skies Tuesday\nnight could again result in some frost. Of note, the growing\nseason for 2026 only remains active east of US-15 as of this \nmorning, so no further frost/freeze headlines will be issued \nwest of there until spring of 2027.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much cooler conditions Thursday through Saturday, along with \nchances for precipitation. \n\nModel guidance is in very good agreement that a very deep trough \nwill dig southward across the Eastern half of the CONUS late in the \nweek. There continues to be pretty good agreement that a longwave \npattern with troughing in the East and ridging in the West will set \nup within the Polar Jet stream, while the subtropical jet looks to \nalso become active across the southeastern CONUS. The big point of \ncontention is how exactly all of these features are going to \ninteract with one another when it comes to impacts in our area. \n\nIn terms of sensible weather, clouds will be on the increase on \nThursday as the upper trough digs to our west. Despite the increase \nin cloud cover, temperatures are still forecast to be seasonable as \nwe'll be in the warm advection ahead of a developing low. Highs are \ngenerally expected to be in the in the upper 50s and lower 60s. An \narea of low pressure is expected to form downstream of the digging \ntrough, but there's still some spread with respect to where exactly \nit will form and how it will track. There is also the biggest \nquestion of if cold air will be in place ahead of this system, or \nwill it lag behind. In mid-November, that can be very hard to \nachieve outside of the mountains, so that will be something to \nreally keep an eye on as we get closer to this system occurring. \nFurther east, right now the pattern seems to favor a rainy solution \nThursday into Friday, and that is reflected in most of the recent \nmodel guidance and their respective ensembles.\n\nAt any rate, the upper low that will pivot over the region late\nFriday and into this weekend and bring in a substantially \ncooler air mass in to the region. It is not out of the question \nto get some rain/snow showers further east into Saturday morning\nunder the upper low, but as of now, am not expecting any \nimpactful accumulations. \n\nThe Alleghenies will have the greatest chance for accumulating snow, \nbut even there, there's still considerable spread, due to the \nuncertainty in the finer scale details within the trough and the \nultimate track/strength of the low. On the back side of the system \nthough, there seems to be a decent shot at some lingering upslope \nsnow showers even into Saturday as well.",
